.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt NQKT 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqkt ,
.Nm nqktini ,
.Nm nqktupd ,
.Nm nqktfin
.Nd KangarooTwelve, the tree hash and extendable-output function
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fo nqkt
.Fa "unsigned bits"
.Fa "const uint8_t *in" "size_t inlen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *custom" "size_t clen"
.Fa "uint8_t *out" "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn nqktini "nqktctx *ctx" "unsigned bits"
.Ft int
.Fn nqktupd "nqktctx *ctx" "const uint8_t *in" "size_t inlen"
.Ft int
.Fo nqktfin
.Fa "nqktctx *ctx"
.Fa "const uint8_t *custom" "size_t clen"
.Fa "uint8_t *out" "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqkt
writes
.Fa outlen
bytes (1 or more) of KangarooTwelve of the
.Fa inlen
bytes at
.Fa in
to
.Fa out :
KT128 when
.Fa bits
is 128, KT256 when it is 256.
KangarooTwelve is an extendable-output function on TurboSHAKE, the
sponge of SHAKE with 12 rounds of Keccak instead of 24: a shorter output
is the start of a longer one.
An input over 8192 bytes is hashed as a tree, each chunk after the first
to a chaining value of its own, which is what makes it fast on large
inputs.
32 bytes of KT128 and 64 of KT256 give their full strength against
collisions, 128 and 256 bits.
.Pp
.Fa custom
is the customization string, a second input that separates one use from
another:
.Dv NULL
with
.Fa clen
0 for none, which is the default of the standard.
With a secret key there the output is a MAC (RFC 9861 section 4):
compare tags with
.Xr nqcteq 3 ,
never
.Xr memcmp 3 .
.Pp
.Fn nqktini ,
.Fn nqktupd
and
.Fn nqktfin
take the input in pieces, for one that is not in memory at once:
.Fn nqktini
once,
.Fn nqktupd
any number of times, and
.Fn nqktfin
once, with the customization string and the output.
The result is that of
.Fn nqkt
on the pieces joined.
.Vt nqktctx
is opaque and may be kept on the stack;
.Fn nqktfin
wipes it when it succeeds, after which
.Fn nqktupd
and
.Fn nqktfin
refuse it until
.Fn nqktini
is called again.
A call that returns
.Dv NQEARG
leaves the context as it was.
.Pp
The time taken depends only on the lengths, never on the data.
Nothing allocates.
.Ss Construction
This is KangarooTwelve of RFC 9861 sections 2 and 3, as computed here.
M is the input,
C the customization string,
L =
.Fa outlen ,
.Sy ||
is concatenation and |X| the length of X in bytes.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
length_encode(x) = x in base 256, most significant byte first,
                   no leading zero bytes (none at all for x = 0),
                   followed by one byte: the number of those bytes
S   = M || C || length_encode(|C|)
KT(M, C, L) = TurboSHAKE(S, 0x07, L)                  if |S| <= 8192
            = TurboSHAKE(FN, 0x06, L)                 otherwise
S   = S_0 || S_1 || ... || S_n         chunks of 8192 bytes, the last
                                       one 1 to 8192 bytes
CV_i = TurboSHAKE(S_i, 0x0B, c)                       i = 1, ..., n
FN  = S_0 || 0x03 || 0x00^7 || CV_1 || ... || CV_n
      || length_encode(n) || 0xFF || 0xFF
.Ed
.Pp
For KT128, TurboSHAKE is TurboSHAKE128 and c = 32; for KT256,
TurboSHAKE256 and c = 64.
TurboSHAKE(X, D, L) is the sponge of FIPS 202 on Keccak-p[1600, 12]
(the last 12 of the 24 rounds of Keccak-f[1600]), with a rate of 168
bytes (TurboSHAKE128) or 136 bytes (TurboSHAKE256): X is absorbed, the
domain byte D is XORed at the next position and 0x80 at the last byte of
the block, and L bytes are squeezed.
A customization string therefore always separates: KT(M, C, L) and
KT(M', C', L) are computed on different strings S unless M = M' and
C = C'.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv NQOK ,
or
.Bl -tag -width NQESTATE
.It Bq Er NQEARG
.Fa bits
is neither 128 nor 256,
.Fa outlen
is 0, or a pointer is
.Dv NULL
with a nonzero length.
.It Bq Er NQESTATE
The context was not made by
.Fn nqktini ,
or
.Fn nqktfin
has ended it.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
uint8_t d[32], buf[65536];
nqktctx c;
size_t n;
int rc = nqkt(128, (const uint8_t *)"abc", 3, NULL, 0, d, sizeof d);

/* a file, in pieces */
rc = nqktini(&c, 128);
while (rc == NQOK && (n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, f)) > 0)
  rc = nqktupd(&c, buf, n);
if (rc == NQOK) rc = nqktfin(&c, NULL, 0, d, sizeof d);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-hash 1 ,
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqb2b 3 ,
.Xr nqcteq 3 ,
.Xr nqhash 3
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 9861; TurboSHAKE builds on the Keccak-p permutation of FIPS 202.
The tests check every test vector of its section 5, those of TurboSHAKE
among them, inputs in pieces cut before, at and after every chunk
boundary, and agreement of KT128 with another implementation on random
inputs.
.Pp
